    "Ultrastructural imaging of collagen fibril organization and D-banding at nanometer resolution",
    "Ultrathin tissue sectioning suitable for electron transmission"
  ],
  "objective_met": true,
  "recommendations": [
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ11",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM)",
      "why": "TEM provides sub-nanometer resolution needed to observe the internal ultrastructure, alignment, and characteristic cross-striation (D-banding) of collagen fibrils.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Digital camera/detector system for digital image acquisition",
        "Standard single-tilt or double-tilt TEM specimen holder",
        "TEM grids (e.g., formvar/carbon-coated copper grids)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ16",
      "role": "supplementary",
      "technique": "Ultramicrotomy",
      "why": "Biological tissues must be cut into ultrathin sections (typically 50–90 nm) to allow electron beam transmission for TEM analysis.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Diamond knife suitable for biological ultrathin sectioning",
        "Specimen holders compatible with embedded resin blocks",
        "Glass knife maker (if glass knives are utilized)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [
    {
      "technique": "Atomic Force Microscopy (AFM)",
      "why_useful": "AFM enables direct, high-resolution topographical imaging and mechanical mapping of individual collagen fibril periodicity in native or hydrated states without resin embedding or heavy metal staining."
    }
  ],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "To visualize collagen fibril ultrastructure at nanometer scale inside tissue, transmission electron microscopy (TEM) using EQ11 is the primary recommended method. Ultrathin sectioning of resin-embedded tissue using the ultramicrotome (EQ16) is necessary as a sample preparation step prior to imaging. Please confirm the availability of an ultramicrotomy diamond knife and appropriate TEM sample holders."
}

{
